        "content": "Web search results for query: \"GitHub Copilot Actions minutes billing change reaction June 2026 developers\"\n\nI'll search for information about GitHub Copilot Actions minutes billing changes and developer reactions in June 2026.**🌐 Z.ai Built-in Tool: web_search_prime**\n\n**Input:**\n```json\n{\"search_query\":\"GitHub Copilot Actions minutes billing change reaction June 2026 developers\"}\n```\n*Executing on server...*\n\n**Output:**\n**web_search_prime_result_summary:** [{\"text\": [{\"title\": \"GitHub Copilot code review will start consuming GitHub Actions ...\", \"link\": \"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-04-27-github-copilot-code-review-will-start-consuming-github-actions-minutes-on-june-1-2026/\", \"content\": \"Starting June 1, 2026, each Copilot code review will be bi...\n                                                Based on the search results, here's a comprehensive overview of the GitHub Copilot billing changes and developer reactions as of June 2026:\n\n## Key Billing Changes (June 1, 2026)\n\n**Major Shift to Usage-Based Billing:**\n- GitHub replaced flat-rate \"Premium Request Units\" with token-based billing called **\"AI Credits\"**\n- **1 AI Credit = $0.01**\n- Copilot code review now consumes **GitHub Actions minutes** in addition to AI Credits\n- All Copilot usage (including code reviews) is billed through this new system\n\n## Developer Reactions & Concerns\n\n**Cost Impact:**\n- Some reports indicate potential cost increases of **up to 800%** for certain use cases\n- Developers on Reddit expressed significant concern about the removal of predictable flat-rate pricing\n- Teams using Actions heavily for CI/CD pipelines face additional costs when code review features are enabled\n\n**Community Response:**\n- Active discussions on GitHub Community forums (#192948, #192963)\n- Reddit threads highlight frustration among developers\n- LinkedIn posts show developers calculating dramatic cost increases (one example cited $1,298.42 for what previously cost much less)\n\n## Resources for Affected Users\n\nSeveral organizations have published guides and FAQs:\n- Cost impact analyses and migration playbooks\n- Detailed FAQs explaining the transition from request-based to usage-based billing\n- Practical breakdowns of what the changes mean for different team sizes\n\n## What Changed\n\n**Previous System:** Flat-rate Premium Request Units\n**New System:** Token-metered AI Credits + Actions minutes consumption for code review features\n\nThe reaction has been notably negative among many developers, particularly those who relied on Copilot for code review workflows integrated with GitHub Actions, as the new billing model introduces variable costs that are difficult to predict and potentially significantly more expensive.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."
